  "title": "Number of equations versus number of unknowns.",
  "body": " Number of equations versus number of unknowns  In your previous mathematical studies, you may have heard the rule of thumb that to solve for variables you need equations .   Use lines in the Cartesian plane to explore whether this rule of thumb is accurate for linear equations in two variables.  Does a system of one linear equation in two unknowns have solutions?  Does a system of two linear equation in two unknowns always have a solution?  Can a system of three linear equations in two unknowns have a solution?    Use planes in three-dimensional space to explore whether this rule of thumb is accurate for linear equations in three variables. (You may wish to use pieces of papers as props to represent different planes.)  Does a system of one linear equation in three unknowns have solutions?  Can a system of two linear equations in three unknowns have solutions?  Does a system of three linear equations in three unknowns always have a solution?  Can a system of four linear equations in three unknowns have a solution?   Based on your reflections above, rewrite the rule of thumb to more accurately express the intended meaning.  "
